  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 535 AM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nThe Northern Plains lie under southwest flow aloft early this \nmorning between a transient ridge exiting the Red River Valley \nand a deep longwave trough over the Pacific Northwest. A nearly-\nstacked low pressure system is depicted on water vapor imagery \nspinning northward through southern Alberta, with a separate \nsurface low analyzed in southeast Montana. A broad expanse of \nlow level warm air advection and moisture transport downstream \nof these features has ignited areas of showers and \nthunderstorms. One cluster of widely scattered showers and \nisolated thunderstorms has been migrating eastward across south \ncentral North Dakota into the James River Valley. Meanwhile, \nclusters of much stronger thunderstorms were moving through \nnorthwest and north central parts of the state as of 5 AM CDT. \nThese strong to at times severe storms are moving through an \nenvironment with up to 2000 J/kg MUCAPE and effective bulk shear\nas high as 40-50 kts. Convective evolution through this \nafternoon is uncertain, and CAMs offer little consistency in \npotential outcomes. The ongoing convection may continue to lift \nnortheastward into southern Canada by this afternoon. Some CAMs \nthen hint at trailing redevelopment in north central North \nDakota this afternoon as an upper level jet streak lifts \nnortheast off the Rockies, but there are obvious questions on \nhow the environment may be modified by morning convection. \nModel soundings and two-dimensional CIN fields suggest a \ncapping inversion is likely to keep storms elevated through the \nday, but UH fields in the HREF broadly support strong to severe \nconvection in north central North Dakota this afternoon with a \nsimilar CAPE/shear parameter space as mentioned above. Storm \nmode could be mixed or clustered as there is no well-defined \nsurface boundary where convection is forecast, so will leave \nour projected hazards at hail up to ping pong ball size and \nwinds up to 60 mph. Torrential rainfall could also caused \nlocalized flooding, as has already been observed in Williston \nand southwest Ward County early this morning.\n\nMid to upper level ridging is forecast to amplify over the \nNorthern Plains on Friday as a deep upper low spins over the \nPacific Northwest. This brings the high temperature forecast \ninto the 90s along and to the south and west of the Missouri \nRiver, along with a strengthening southeast surface flow around\n20 to 25 mph. In western North Dakota, RH is forecast to fall \nbelow 30 percent south of the Missouri River, with minimum \nvalues closer to 15 percent in the far southwest along the \nMontana border. The greatest overlap in strong winds and low RH \nis in Slope and Bowman Counties, where a Fire Weather Watch has \nbeen issued for Friday afternoon and early evening. Surrounding \nareas in west central and southwest North Dakota could see near-\ncritical fire weather conditions on Friday.\n\nThe ridge is forecast to become even more amplified on \nSaturday, with forecast highs ranging from the mid and upper \n80s north to lower and mid 90s south. The low level jet could \ninduce convection over parts of the region late Friday night \ninto Saturday morning, but mostly dry weather is otherwise \nanticipated from tonight until Saturday night when the deep \nwestern CONUS trough begins kicking shortwave energy into the \nNorthern Plains. This culminates in widespread low to medium \nshower and thunderstorm chances Sunday through Labor Day as a \nmore potent shortwave lifts northeastward over the region while\nthe original Pacific Northwest low spins toward the Canadian \nRockies. Temperatures are favored to trend cooler early to mid \nnext week as the upper low crosses southern Canada.",
